```js
const cache = new Map();
const TTL = 60 * 60 * 1000; // 1 hour

async function getWeather(city) {
  const key = city.trim().toLowerCase();
  const cached = cache.get(key);

  if (cached && Date.now() < cached.expires) {
    return cached.data;
  }

  const response = await fetch(
    `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${encodeURIComponent(city)}&units=metric&appid=YOUR_API_KEY`
  );

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Weather API error: HTTP ${response.status}`);
  }

  const data = await response.json();
  cache.set(key, { data, expires: Date.now() + TTL });
  return data;
}
```

This is dependency-free and keeps the app lightweight. The cache resets when the page reloads. If persistence across sessions is needed, store `{ data, timestamp }` in `localStorage`, remove expired entries, and consider limiting the number of cached cities. A longer TTL, such as one hour, also helps stay within the 1,000-call daily limit.

